const faq = [
  { q: 'Was the raven originally white in mythology?', a: 'In several independent mythological traditions, the raven was originally white (or some colour other than black) and was turned black as a consequence of a transgression. The most famous Western version is the Greek myth of Apollo\'s white raven, cursed black for bringing bad news. Aboriginal Australian traditions from southeastern Australia describe Crow turning black after being scorched stealing fire. This "originally white" motif is a widespread narrative explaining the raven\'s conspicuous black colour.' },
  { q: 'What is the Greek myth about the raven turning black?', a: 'In the Greek tradition recorded by Ovid (Metamorphoses, Book II), Apollo kept a white raven (or crow — the distinction is not always clear in ancient texts) as a messenger. When the bird reported that Apollo\'s beloved Coronis had been unfaithful, Apollo killed Coronis in rage, then in his grief cursed the raven and turned its feathers black — blaming the messenger for bringing him the news of his sorrow. The raven was thereafter black. This myth is an aetiological explanation (an origin story) for the raven\'s colour.' },
  { q: 'Why are ravens actually black?', a: 'Ravens are black because of melanin — specifically eumelanin, a dark pigment produced by melanocyte cells in the skin and deposited in feather barbules during feather growth. Black plumage in corvids is ancestral; the birds are not "dyed" black by any event but evolved with melanin-rich plumage. Black feathers contain more eumelanin than lighter feathers, making them structurally stronger and more resistant to abrasion and bacterial degradation — a potential functional benefit. The iridescent blue-green-purple sheen of raven feathers in sunlight is produced by the nanostructure of the feather surface interacting with light (structural colour), overlaid on the black base colour.' },
];
